/* Fact-checked article and FAQ content. Source reviewed on Hipobuy's public guides. */
(() => {
  const page = document.body.dataset.page;
  const app = document.getElementById('app');
  if (!app) return;
  const article = `
    <section class="section alt"><div class="heading"><div class="eyebrow">SEO ARTICLE · PRACTICAL GUIDE</div><h1>How to Use HipoBuy Carefully: From Product Link to International Parcel</h1><p>A fact-led guide for buyers who want to understand the workflow before they pay.</p></div></section>
    <article class="section longform"><p><strong>HipoBuy is a shopping-agent service, not a single retail shop.</strong> Its public home page describes a workflow built around a product link, a warehouse in China and a later international-parcel decision. That distinction is the best place to begin. When you paste a product link, you are asking an agent to help purchase an item from a third-party seller. The listing, seller, domestic delivery, warehouse handling and international route can all affect the outcome. A sensible buyer therefore treats the first displayed item price as one part of a larger decision, rather than as a final delivered cost.</p>
    <h2>1. Start with a link that can be checked</h2><p>HipoBuy’s search field accepts a product link, a product name or a HipoBuy order number, and its home page also offers Custom Order and Forwarding Order options. For a normal purchase, the cleanest starting point is a complete product URL and a written note of the exact variant: colour, size, quantity and any seller message that matters. Do this before paying. A screenshot can help you remember what you selected, but it is not a replacement for reviewing the live listing. Stock, seller prices, delivery fees and options can move before an agent completes the purchase.</p><p>Before creating the order, compare the title, product photos, options and seller reputation where those details are available. If the listing is ambiguous, ask a precise question through the order process instead of relying on a broad label such as “same as photo.” This is especially important for shoes, clothing and accessories, where a variation can change materials, sizing or packaging. The useful habit is to document what you requested early, so that later warehouse photos and any after-sales discussion have something concrete to compare against.</p>
    <h2>2. Understand the warehouse stage</h2><p>HipoBuy says that products bought from different merchants are shipped to its warehouse in China. Its public service description also says that, after an item arrives, it conducts acceptance and quality inspection and provides the result. This is valuable because the warehouse stage happens before you decide on an international parcel. It is not, however, a promise that every quality issue will be visible or that an item will fit your personal preference. Treat the inspection result as evidence to review, not as a guarantee of product performance.</p><p>When an item is marked warehoused, check the identity of the item first: product type, count, colour, size label and obvious condition. Then compare the visible details with the original order note. Look for practical warning signs such as an obviously wrong colour, missing accessories, damaged outer packaging or a size label that does not match the selected option. If something appears wrong, raise the issue while the product is still in the warehouse. Waiting until the parcel has been consolidated or shipped internationally can reduce the options available to you.</p>
    <h2>3. The return window is a real deadline</h2><p>HipoBuy’s Returns and Refunds guide describes a “5-Day Return Guarantee” for eligible products. The operational timing is more specific than the name alone: the guide says a return request can be made within five days after an order becomes “Warehoused,” starting from the next hour after that status changes, with five days treated as 120 hours. A request may not be accepted after the period expires. That is why buyers should not postpone reviewing warehouse information simply because international shipping will happen later.</p><p>Eligibility is not automatic. According to the published guide, the seller must offer a return guarantee, the product must remain in a marketable condition required by the seller, and the warehouse inventory time must not exceed five days. The guide also lists common exclusions or restrictions, including customised items, items with removed packaging, tags or accessories, second-hand products, some personal items such as underwear, socks and leggings, food and certain sealed or special-standard products. These examples are useful reminders that a product can be physically present in the warehouse yet still be unsuitable for a routine return.</p><p>Costs also matter. For a buyer-requested, no-fault return, HipoBuy’s guide states a formula of shipping back to the seller, the seller’s original shipping cost and a 5 RMB service fee. It notes that an original “free shipping” offer does not necessarily mean the first domestic dispatch cost disappears when a return is requested. If a seller sent the wrong item or the item has a verified quality issue, responsibility may differ, but the final result still depends on the seller and the actual case. Do not describe any return as free until the order screen and the seller’s position confirm it.</p>
    <h2>4. Consolidation is a choice, not a shortcut</h2><p>HipoBuy says stored products can be freely combined into individual parcels, then sent through a logistics provider available on the platform. Consolidation can make sense when several purchases have arrived, but it also creates a point where the parcel’s size, weight and category become more important than the price of any one item. The buyer should decide what belongs together only after reviewing the warehouse records. Do not consolidate a questionable item merely to save a small amount of time.</p><p>Make a parcel plan: list each item, its variant, whether it has been checked, and whether it has any category-specific shipping risk. Then consider the destination, the declared contents and the route choices displayed for that parcel. A route that looks cheaper before packing may not be the best option once dimensions, service limits or destination rules are known. HipoBuy provides a Shipping Estimate / Charges entry point; use the current estimator for the actual destination and parcel details rather than copying a price from an old post or another buyer’s order.</p>
    <h2>5. Do not promise a fixed final cost or delivery time</h2><p>International freight is calculated after the goods and parcel details are known, and the available logistics choices can vary. For that reason, a responsible HipoBuy guide should not promise a universal shipping price, a fixed number of delivery days or a customs outcome. The productive approach is to budget in stages: product price, domestic purchase and handling where shown, any return risk, then the international parcel estimate. Keep a margin for changes in dimensions, route availability, taxes or destination processing.</p><p>Customs and taxation are destination-specific. HipoBuy links to a dedicated Customs and Taxation guide, but buyers should also check their own country’s import rules. An agent can provide service options; it cannot remove local legal obligations. Be accurate about the contents of a parcel, read the selected route’s terms, and avoid treating online advice as a substitute for local customs information. This is not pessimism; it is the practical way to avoid turning a low product price into a surprise total cost.</p>
    <h2>6. A safer order checklist</h2><p>Before payment, save the product link and exact variant. At warehouse arrival, review the acceptance and quality-inspection result promptly. If you need a return, check whether the product is eligible and act within the 120-hour window instead of assuming that any item can be sent back later. Before parcel submission, verify the list of items, evaluate whether consolidation is sensible, and use HipoBuy’s current estimator for the destination. Finally, keep order records and support messages until the parcel is received.</p><p>The point of this workflow is not to make shopping complicated. It is to place each decision at the stage where it can still be changed. HipoBuy’s own public information describes product-link purchasing, warehouse acceptance and inspection, after-sales handling, parcel combination and selectable logistics providers. Used carefully, those stages give a buyer several opportunities to check an order. Used casually, they can blur into one expensive decision. Review early, document clearly and rely on the live order and policy pages for the final terms.</p>
    <p class="source-note">Editorial note: This guide is based on HipoBuy’s public home-page service description and its public Returns and Refunds and Charges guides, reviewed September 2026. Policies, route availability and charges can change; the live HipoBuy order and policy pages control.</p></article>`;
  const faq = `
    <section class="section alt"><div class="heading"><div class="eyebrow">FACT-LED FAQ</div><h1>HipoBuy: practical questions before you submit a parcel</h1><p>Answers are based on public HipoBuy guidance and avoid fixed promises where the live order determines the result.</p></div></section>
    <section class="section faq-list longform">
      <details open><summary>What does HipoBuy do after I submit a product link?</summary><p>HipoBuy describes itself as assisting with purchases from third-party online and offline channels in China. Its public flow is product purchase, warehouse arrival, acceptance and quality inspection, then parcel selection and international shipping.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I buy from more than one seller?</summary><p>Yes. HipoBuy says products from different merchants can be sent to its warehouse. Once stored, buyers can choose which items to combine into individual parcels.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Are warehouse inspection results a guarantee of quality?</summary><p>No. HipoBuy states that it conducts acceptance and quality inspection and provides the result. Review the available evidence yourself: it can help identify visible mismatches or defects, but it cannot guarantee fit, materials, authenticity or long-term performance.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How long do I have to request an eligible return?</summary><p>HipoBuy’s published guide says to request the service within five days after the order becomes “Warehoused,” counted from the next hour after the status changes; it describes five days as 120 hours.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Is every warehoused item eligible for a return?</summary><p>No. The guide says the seller must offer the guarantee, the item must meet the seller’s marketable-condition requirement, and its warehouse time must not exceed five days. Customised goods, items with removed tags or packaging, and several special categories can be ineligible.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Are socks and underwear covered by the routine return guarantee?</summary><p>HipoBuy’s public exclusions list includes underwear, socks and leggings. Always check the specific item and live order status because seller rules and product conditions also matter.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What can a no-fault return cost?</summary><p>HipoBuy’s guide lists domestic shipping back to the seller, the seller’s original shipping cost and a 5 RMB service fee for an unconditional return. The exact charge can depend on the case and seller; check the live return request before confirming.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I return an item after it has been sent internationally?</summary><p>Do not assume so. The published warehouse return process is tied to the warehoused stage and a short deadline. Once a parcel is submitted or delivered internationally, the relevant after-sales path and available remedies can be different.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How is international shipping priced?</summary><p>HipoBuy directs buyers to its current Shipping Estimate / Charges page. Use the estimator with the actual destination and parcel information; do not rely on a fixed price or another buyer’s historical quote.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I promise a delivery date before the parcel is packed?</summary><p>No. Logistics options and final parcel details affect the route. A responsible plan is to use the live estimate, allow for carrier and customs variability, and avoid dates that are not stated by the selected service.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Who is responsible for import taxes and customs rules?</summary><p>Destination rules vary. Read the current HipoBuy route information and your local customs guidance before shipping. An agent service does not remove the buyer’s responsibility to comply with destination requirements.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What is the most useful first-time-buyer habit?</summary><p>Keep a clear record of the product link, exact variant and order notes; review warehouse evidence promptly; and make the parcel decision only after every item you plan to ship has been checked.</p></details>
      <p class="source-note">Source basis: HipoBuy’s public service overview, Returns and Refunds guide, and Charges / Shipping Estimate entry, reviewed September 2026. Live terms control.</p>
    </section>`;
  const applyEditorial = () => {
    if (page === 'articles') app.innerHTML = article;
    if (page === 'faq') app.innerHTML = faq;
    if (page === 'home' && !app.querySelector('[data-full-seo-article]')) {
      app.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend', '<section class="section alt compact" data-full-seo-article><div class="heading"><div class="eyebrow">SEO ARTICLE · PRACTICAL GUIDE</div><h2>How to Use HipoBuy Carefully: From Product Link to International Parcel</h2><p>The complete fact-led guide is available here on the homepage.</p></div><details class="mobile-article-fold"><summary>Read the complete article</summary>' + article.replace(/^\s*<section[\s\S]*?<\/section>\s*/, '').replace('<article class="section longform">', '<article class="longform">') + '</details></section>');
    }
    app.querySelectorAll('.mobile-article-fold').forEach(fold=>{fold.open=!window.matchMedia('(max-width:760px)').matches;});
  };
  applyEditorial();
  const language = document.getElementById('language');
  if (language) language.addEventListener('change', () => setTimeout(applyEditorial, 0));
})();

